    <section class="section" id="advanced">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced</span>
      <h2>Advanced Workflows</h2>
      <p>VibeCrew includes four advanced workflows that extend the development lifecycle: reusable session templates, automated code simplification, self-healing CI, and adversarial review of technology decisions.</p>

      <!-- REPLAY -->
      <h3 id="replay">Workflow Templates (<code>/replay</code>)</h3>
      <p>Capture what worked and reuse it. <code>/replay</code> extracts reusable workflow templates from successful sessions (Vibe Score &ge; 70) so you can apply proven patterns to future features.</p>

      <h4>Three modes</h4>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">

          <h4>List templates</h4>
          <p>Run <code>/replay</code> with no arguments to list all saved workflow templates with their names, descriptions, and quality thresholds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Create a template</h4>
          <p>Run <code>/replay --create &lt;name&gt;</code> to extract a template from the most recent high-scoring session. Captures phase order, branch conventions, test strategy, and quality thresholds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Apply a template</h4>
          <p>Run <code>/replay &lt;name&gt;</code> to load a template and have it guide your current feature development with the proven workflow.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout type="tip" title="When to create templates">
        After a session scores 90+, create a template. Common examples: "api-feature" for CRUD endpoints, "ui-component" for design-system-aligned components, "data-migration" for schema changes. Templates are stored in <code>.vibecrew/workflows/</code>.
      </Callout>

      <!-- SIMPLIFY -->
      <h3 id="simplify">Code Simplification (<code>/simplify</code>)</h3>
      <p>After building a feature, run <code>/simplify</code> to get an Opus-powered analysis of your code for unnecessary complexity. The agent operates in a read-only worktree &mdash; it analyzes but never modifies your code directly.</p>

      <h4>The simplification cycle</h4>
      <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">/simplify</span> collects files changed on the feature branch
    &darr;
Launches the <span class="highlight">Code Simplifier</span> agent (Opus, worktree, read-only)
    &darr;
Agent analyzes four categories:
  <span class="highlight">Dead code</span> &middot; <span class="highlight">Abstraction flattening</span> &middot; <span class="highlight">API simplification</span> &middot; <span class="highlight">Dependency reduction</span>
    &darr;
Presents suggestions one at a time for your approval
    &darr;
<span class="muted">Each accepted change is applied and tests re-run</span>
<span class="muted">If tests fail &rarr; automatic revert</span></div>

      <p>Suggestions are categorized by impact (<strong>high</strong>, <strong>medium</strong>, <strong>low</strong>) and include before/after snippets, estimated line reduction, and confidence ratings. You approve or reject each individually. Reports are saved to <code>.vibecrew/simplifications/</code>.</p>

      <Callout type="info" title="Safe by design">
        The analysis agent cannot write files. Only the <code>/simplify</code> skill applies approved changes in the main session, and every change is verified by running your test suite. Failed tests trigger an automatic revert.
      </Callout>

      <!-- HEAL -->
      <h3 id="heal">Auto-Healing CI (<code>/heal</code>)</h3>
      <p>When CI fails, run <code>/heal</code> instead of manually debugging logs. The CI Healer fetches the failure logs from GitHub Actions or GitLab CI pipelines, diagnoses the root cause, and applies targeted fixes &mdash; up to 3 attempts.</p>

      <h4>How it works</h4>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h4>Fetch and diagnose</h4>
          <p><code>/heal</code> runs <code>gh run view --log-failed</code> (GitHub) or <code>glab ci view</code> (GitLab) to retrieve the CI output, then pattern-matches errors to categorize the failure: <strong>build</strong>, <strong>test</strong>, <strong>lint</strong>, <strong>dependency</strong>, or <strong>environment</strong>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h4>Checkpoint</h4>
          <p>Before any fix, a git checkpoint is created so all changes can be reverted cleanly if the fix doesn't work.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h4>Fix loop (max 3 attempts)</h4>
          <p>The CI Healer agent applies the minimum viable fix for the diagnosed failure, then pushes and waits for CI. If CI fails again, it re-diagnoses with the new context and tries a different approach.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h4>Report</h4>
          <p>A heal report is saved to <code>.vibecrew/ci-heals/</code> documenting each attempt, diagnosis, fix, and outcome. If all 3 attempts fail, the healer escalates to you with a summary of what it tried.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <TableWrap>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Failure Category</th><th>Common Patterns</th><th>Fix Strategy</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Build</strong></td><td>TypeScript errors, missing imports, syntax errors</td><td>Fix the type error or import at its source</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Test</strong></td><td>Assertion failures, snapshot mismatches, timeouts</td><td>Update assertion/snapshot or fix the logic bug</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lint</strong></td><td>ESLint/Prettier violations</td><td>Auto-fix with the project's lint command</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Dependency</strong></td><td>Missing packages, version conflicts</td><td>Install missing dep or resolve version conflict</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Environment</strong></td><td>Missing env vars, wrong Node version, permission errors</td><td>Update CI config (YAML/TOML) to match requirements</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableWrap>

      <!-- ISSUE TRACKER -->
      <h3 id="issue-tracker">Issue Tracker Integration (<code>/fix-issue</code>)</h3>
      <p>Connect GitHub Issues or GitLab Issues directly to VibeCrew&rsquo;s development pipeline. Someone files a bug, a human labels it for auto-fix, and VibeCrew diagnoses, implements, tests, and opens a PR or MR &mdash; all autonomously. The provider is auto-detected from the git remote URL, with a config override in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code>.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">

          <h4><code>/fix-issue &lt;number&gt;</code></h4>
          <p>Interactive single-issue fix. Fetches the issue from GitHub or GitLab, imports it into the backlog, creates a branch, implements the fix with the Builder agent, runs quality checks, and opens a PR/MR with <code>Fixes #N</code> (or <code>Closes #N</code> on GitLab) for auto-close.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4><code>/sync-issues</code></h4>
          <p>Batch-import open issues by label (default: <code>autofix</code>) from GitHub or GitLab into the backlog. Zero token cost &mdash; pure bash. Then run <code>/run-backlog</code> to process them automatically.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>CI Automation</h4>
          <p>Fully automated CI-triggered fixes. On GitHub, the <code>github-actions-autofix.yml</code> template triggers on <code>issues.labeled</code> events. On GitLab, the <code>gitlab-ci-autofix.yml.template</code> provides equivalent pipeline-triggered fixes. Both run Claude Code CLI headless and comment results back on the issue.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h4>Hotfix vs Full Feature</h4>
      <p>Bug issues use a <strong>hotfix path</strong> (Code &rarr; Test &rarr; Review) that skips planning and design since bugs don&rsquo;t need upfront design work. Issues labeled <code>enhancement</code> or <code>feature-request</code>, or those invoked with <code>--full</code>, use the standard 6-phase Tier 2 cycle.</p>

      <Callout type="tip" title="Deduplication built in">
        The import script tracks <code>issue_number</code> on each backlog entry. Running <code>/sync-issues</code> multiple times or calling <code>/fix-issue</code> on an already-imported issue will detect the duplicate and offer to resume instead of creating a new entry.
      </Callout>

      <!-- OPPONENT -->
      <h3 id="opponent">Opponent Processor (TDR Debate)</h3>
      <p>Every technology decision deserves a devil's advocate. During Tier 1, after the Stack Scout produces the TDR, VibeCrew automatically invokes the <strong>Opponent Processor</strong> &mdash; a read-only agent that challenges every decision.</p>

      <h4>What it produces</h4>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Counter-arguments</h4>
          <p>For each technology decision, the opponent generates 2&ndash;3 reasons why the choice might be wrong, citing real-world failure modes and edge cases.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Debate matrix</h4>
          <p>A structured comparison table scoring each option across 6 criteria: performance, DX, ecosystem, scalability, learning curve, and lock-in risk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Risk assessment</h4>
          <p>Identifies the highest-risk decisions and recommends which ones deserve extra scrutiny or prototyping before committing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>After reviewing both the TDR and the counter-analysis, you choose: <strong>keep the original decisions</strong>, <strong>reconsider specific choices</strong>, or <strong>skip</strong> the review. The opponent's analysis is saved alongside the TDR for future reference.</p>

      <Callout type="tip" title="Why adversarial review matters">
        The Stack Scout is optimized to find the best answer quickly. The Opponent Processor is optimized to find where that answer might break. Together, they produce more robust technology decisions than either could alone. This is especially valuable for decisions that are expensive to reverse (database choice, state management, auth provider).
      </Callout>

      <!-- INTER-FEATURE CONTEXT HYGIENE -->
      <h3 id="context-hygiene">Inter-Feature Context Hygiene (<code>/run-backlog</code>)</h3>
      <p>When <code>/run-backlog</code> processes multiple features autonomously, stale conversation history from earlier features can degrade quality for later features. VibeCrew solves this with <strong>fresh-session Agent delegation</strong>.</p>

      <p><strong>Primary approach:</strong> Each feature's build phases (Plan&rarr;Test) are spawned as an Agent tool call with a completely fresh 200k context window. The <code>prepare-feature-context.sh</code> script assembles the feature spec, TDR, architecture diagrams, design system, CLAUDE.md, user profile, and codebase analysis docs into a structured prompt. The Agent runs in its own worktree, eliminating context rot entirely.</p>

      <p><strong>Fallback:</strong> If Agent delegation is unavailable, VibeCrew falls back to inline execution with <code>/compact</code> between features. This compresses stale history and fires <code>compact-reinject.sh</code> to re-inject fresh project state and architecture diagrams.</p>

      <Callout type="tip" title="Impact on long autonomous runs">
        With fresh-session execution, feature 10 gets the exact same 200k context quality as feature 1. The orchestrating session only accumulates lightweight state management overhead. This is a significant improvement over compaction, which still carries summarized prior context. See <a href="runtime.html">Runtime State</a> for the full technical flow.
      </Callout>

      <!-- WAVE-BASED MILESTONES -->
      <h3 id="wave-milestones">Wave-Based Milestones (Complex Features)</h3>
      <p>Complex features with milestones can now declare <strong>dependencies</strong> between milestones via the <code>depends_on</code> field. Independent milestones are grouped into <strong>waves</strong> and executed in parallel.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Dependency Analysis</h4>
          <p>During the Plan phase, the Builder analyzes shared files and data flow between milestones to populate <code>depends_on</code>. Milestones sharing files or where one produces data another consumes must have explicit dependencies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Wave Computation</h4>
          <p><code>compute-milestone-waves.sh</code> performs topological sort to group milestones into waves. Wave 1 milestones have no dependencies and can run in parallel. Wave 2 depends on Wave 1, etc.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Parallel Execution</h4>
          <p>Independent milestones in a wave spawn parallel Agent calls, each with its own worktree and fresh context. After a wave completes, <code>merge-milestone-branches.sh</code> merges the results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Safety Fallbacks</h4>
          <p>Cycle detection falls back to sequential. Merge conflicts are resolved by file ownership from <code>estimated_files</code>. If all milestones are linear, behavior is unchanged from sequential processing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STRUCTURED TASK PLANS -->
      <h3 id="structured-tasks">Structured Task Plans</h3>
      <p>Implementation plans now include a <strong>structured <code>## Tasks</code> section</strong> alongside the existing free-form sections (Approach, Files, Component Strategy, etc.). Each task has explicit fields:</p>

      <pre><code>### Task 1: Create database schema
- <strong>Files</strong>: `prisma/schema.prisma` (modify)
- <strong>Action</strong>: Add User and Session models with email, password_hash, created_at
- <strong>Verify</strong>: `npx prisma validate`
- <strong>Done when</strong>: Schema validates without errors and includes both models</code></pre>

      <p>The Code phase extracts structured tasks via <code>extract-plan-tasks.sh</code> and processes them sequentially: implement the Action, run the Verify command, check the Done criteria, commit per task. Legacy plans without structured tasks fall back to free-form execution. Task count scales with complexity: 2-3 for trivial, 4-6 for standard, 6-8 per milestone for complex.</p>

      <!-- PLAN FRESHNESS CHECKS -->
      <h3 id="plan-freshness">Plan Freshness Checks</h3>
      <p>When multiple features execute sequentially (especially during <code>/run-backlog</code>), earlier features may modify files that later features planned against. VibeCrew records the git SHA when each plan completes and checks for drift before the Code phase starts.</p>

      <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Plan phase completes &rarr; git SHA recorded in <code>state.json</code></li>
        <li>Code phase starts &rarr; <code>check-plan-staleness.sh</code> diffs plan files against recorded SHA</li>
        <li>Severity determines response: proceed, review, or refresh affected tasks</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Severity levels:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Minor</strong> (1&ndash;2 files, &lt;20 lines): Re-read affected files, proceed</li>
        <li><strong>Major</strong> (3+ files or &gt;50 lines): Refresh affected plan tasks</li>
        <li><strong>Critical</strong> (deleted/renamed files): Mandatory re-plan of affected tasks</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Behavior adapts to your autonomy setting &mdash; <code>full_auto</code> refreshes silently, <code>collaborative</code> asks before refreshing, <code>supervised</code> shows full diffs.</p>

      <!-- PERSISTENT CODEBASE ANALYSIS -->
      <h3 id="analysis-docs">Persistent Codebase Analysis</h3>
      <p>When you onboard an existing project with <code>/onboard</code>, VibeCrew generates <strong>4 persistent analysis documents</strong> in <code>.vibecrew/analysis/</code> that capture your codebase's stack, architecture, conventions, and gaps. These docs are auto-injected into every Plan phase and post-compaction context, eliminating the need to re-discover conventions each session.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>stack.md</code></h4>
          <p>Language, framework, runtime, package manager, key dependencies, component library, database, deployment target.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>architecture.md</code></h4>
          <p>Source directories, component organization, API routes, schema location, state management, error handling, auth approach.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>conventions.md</code></h4>
          <p>Code style (quotes, semicolons, indent), formatter/linter, naming patterns, import style, commit format, test framework.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>gaps.md</code></h4>
          <p>Source vs test file count, estimated coverage, untested modules (top 10), deprecated dependencies, TODO count.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Analysis docs are <strong>committed to git</strong> (not gitignored) and persist across sessions and team members. Staleness detection runs on session startup &mdash; if docs are &gt;30 days old with &gt;50 commits, or <code>package.json</code> changed, VibeCrew warns you. Refresh with <code>/onboard --refresh</code> (re-runs the auditor without re-generating CLAUDE.md or re-initializing state).</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section class="section" id="cost-system">
      <span class="section-label">Cost &amp; Safety</span>
      <h2>Cost Tracking &amp; Safety</h2>
      <p>VibeCrew provides real-time cost visibility and multi-layered safety enforcement. Cost tracking runs at zero token cost via hook scripts, and safety hooks block destructive operations before they execute.</p>

      <!-- COST DASHBOARD -->
      <h3 id="cost-dashboard">Cost Dashboard (<code>/cost</code>)</h3>
      <p>Run <code>/cost</code> at any time to see a complete financial picture of your AI usage.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Current Session</h4>
          <p>Live cost, turn count, model name, and last-updated timestamp. Read from <code>.vibecrew/session-cost.json</code> which is updated after every assistant turn.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Aggregates</h4>
          <p>Daily, weekly, monthly, and all-time totals calculated by <code>aggregate-costs.sh</code> from session log files in <code>.vibecrew/sessions/</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Threshold Status</h4>
          <p>Visual progress bars showing proximity to session warning, session max, and daily warning limits. Configurable in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">

          <h4>Pricing Reference</h4>
          <p>Per-model pricing table for Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku showing input, cache write, cache read, and output costs per million tokens.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h4>Model pricing (per million tokens)</h4>
      <TableWrap>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>Input</th><th>Cache Write</th><th>Cache Read</th><th>Output</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Opus</strong></td><td>$15.00</td><td>$18.75</td><td>$1.50</td><td>$75.00</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Sonnet</strong></td><td>$3.00</td><td>$3.75</td><td>$0.30</td><td>$15.00</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Haiku</strong></td><td>$0.25</td><td>$0.30</td><td>$0.03</td><td>$1.25</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableWrap>

      <!-- COST GUARDRAILS -->
      <h3 id="cost-guardrails">Cost Guardrails</h3>
      <p>The <code>cost-guardrails.sh</code> hook runs on every Stop event, calculating per-turn costs with model-aware pricing and accumulating totals in <code>.vibecrew/session-cost.json</code>. Alongside cost monitoring, the Stop hook also runs <code>quality-gate.sh</code>, which detects modified source files and runs typecheck/lint/build checks automatically. See <a href="hooks.html">Hooks</a> for details.</p>

      <TableWrap>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Default</th><th>Config Key</th><th>Action</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Session Warning</strong></td>
              <td>$2.00</td>
              <td><code>cost_limits.session_warn_usd</code></td>
              <td>Emits a cost warning to the agent</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Session Hard Limit</strong></td>
              <td>$5.00</td>
              <td><code>cost_limits.session_max_usd</code></td>
              <td>Agent MUST pause and ask for permission to continue</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Daily Warning</strong></td>
              <td>$20.00</td>
              <td><code>cost_limits.daily_warn_usd</code></td>
              <td>Emits a daily spend warning</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableWrap>

      <p>Daily totals are calculated by summing <code>estimated_cost_usd</code> from all session files matching today&rsquo;s date, plus the live session cost. All thresholds are configurable in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code>.</p>

      <!-- SAFETY HOOKS -->
      <h3 id="safety-hooks">Safety Hooks</h3>
      <p>VibeCrew enforces safety through PreToolUse hooks (<code>protect-data.sh</code>, <code>phase-gate.sh</code>, <code>restrict-paths.sh</code>) and permission rules in <code>settings.json</code>. These block destructive commands, enforce foundation-before-code ordering, and restrict file writes to the project directory &mdash; all at zero token cost.</p>
      <p>See the <a href="safety.html">Permissions &amp; Safety</a> page for the full breakdown of hooks, permission rules, and enforcement layers.</p>

      <Callout type="warn" title="Checkpoint safety net">
        Both <code>/heal</code> and <code>/new-feature</code> create a git checkpoint (<code>vibecrew-checkpoint-&lt;timestamp&gt;</code>) before making changes. If anything goes wrong, <code>/undo</code> can roll back to any checkpoint. Checkpoints are lightweight git tags that don&rsquo;t add repository overhead.
      </Callout>

      <!-- SECURITY AUDIT -->
      <h3 id="security-audit">Security Audit (<code>/audit</code>)</h3>
      <p>Run <code>/audit</code> to perform an OWASP Top 10 security analysis of your project. The Security Auditor agent operates in a <strong>read-only worktree</strong> &mdash; it analyzes your code but never modifies it.</p>

      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Injection vulnerabilities</strong> &mdash; SQL injection, command injection, XSS</li>
        <li><strong>Authentication &amp; authorization</strong> &mdash; missing auth checks, insecure session handling</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets exposure</strong> &mdash; hardcoded API keys, tokens, credentials in source</li>
        <li><strong>Dependency vulnerabilities</strong> &mdash; runs language-specific audit tools (<code>npm audit</code>, <code>pip audit</code>, etc.)</li>
        <li><strong>Security headers &amp; configuration</strong> &mdash; missing CORS, CSP, HSTS headers</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Findings are categorized by severity (critical, high, medium, low) and saved to <code>.vibecrew/</code>. The audit adapts to your project's language &mdash; see <a href="#multi-lang">Multi-Language Support</a> for details.</p>

      <!-- MORE COMMANDS -->
      <h3 id="more-commands">More Commands</h3>
      <p>These commands cover specialized workflows. Each is a single slash command that orchestrates the right agent(s) and scripts.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/handoff</code></h4>
          <p>Generates a structured context transfer document capturing in-progress work, decisions made, and next steps. Use when context is running low or you need to continue in a new session.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/pause</code></h4>
          <p>Checkpoints the active feature: commits WIP changes, creates a pause handoff file, cleans up the worktree, and preserves the branch. Use when you need to switch context mid-feature.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/resume</code></h4>
          <p>Resumes a previously paused feature: restores state, recreates the worktree from the preserved branch, and continues at the paused phase. Session Startup auto-detects paused features.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/release</code></h4>
          <p>Automates version bumps, changelog generation from conventional commits, and release notes creation. The Doc Generator compiles a release summary from git history and session logs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/tdd</code></h4>
          <p>Test-driven development workflow: write a failing test first, then implement until it passes, then refactor. The Verifier agent guides the red-green-refactor cycle.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/debug</code></h4>
          <p>Structured debugging with hypothesis testing. The Builder agent systematically isolates the issue, forms hypotheses, tests each one, and applies the fix.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/e2e</code></h4>
          <p>Generates Playwright end-to-end tests using the Page Object Model pattern. Tests cover user flows defined in the feature spec and run in real browsers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/perf-test</code></h4>
          <p>Generates k6 performance test suites covering load, stress, spike, and soak scenarios. Establishes baselines for response times and throughput.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4><code>/a11y</code></h4>
          <p>Runs WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility scanning via axe-core. Reports violations by severity with fix suggestions. Contributes +2 to Vibe Score when clean.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section class="section" id="multi-lang">
      <span class="section-label">Language Support</span>
      <h2>Multi-Language Support</h2>
      <p>While VibeCrew was designed with JavaScript/TypeScript projects in mind, it supports 7 languages for onboarding, convention detection, security auditing, and dependency scanning.</p>

      <TableWrap>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Language</th><th>Manifest</th><th>Formatter</th><th>Linter</th><th>Framework Detection</th><th>Dep Audit</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Node.js / TypeScript</strong></td>
              <td><code>package.json</code></td>
              <td>Prettier</td>
              <td>ESLint</td>
              <td>React, Next.js, Vue, Svelte, Express, Prisma</td>
              <td><code>npm audit</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Python</strong></td>
              <td><code>pyproject.toml</code></td>
              <td>Black, Ruff</td>
              <td>Ruff, Flake8</td>
              <td>Django, Flask, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy</td>
              <td><code>pip audit</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Ruby</strong></td>
              <td><code>Gemfile</code></td>
              <td>RuboCop</td>
              <td>RuboCop</td>
              <td>Rails, ActiveRecord, Devise</td>
              <td><code>bundle audit</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Go</strong></td>
              <td><code>go.mod</code></td>
              <td>gofmt</td>
              <td>golangci-lint</td>
              <td>Gin, Echo, Fiber, GORM</td>
              <td><code>govulncheck</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Rust</strong></td>
              <td><code>Cargo.toml</code></td>
              <td>rustfmt</td>
              <td>clippy</td>
              <td>Actix, Axum, Diesel, SeaORM</td>
              <td><code>cargo audit</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>PHP</strong></td>
              <td><code>composer.json</code></td>
              <td>PHP-CS-Fixer</td>
              <td>PHPStan</td>
              <td>Laravel, Symfony, Eloquent, Doctrine</td>
              <td><code>composer audit</code></td>
            </tr>
