              <td>&mdash;</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableWrap>

      <h3>How language detection works</h3>
      <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">detect-conventions.sh</span> scans project root for manifests
    &darr;
Identifies language from: <span class="highlight">package.json</span>, <span class="highlight">pyproject.toml</span>, <span class="highlight">Gemfile</span>, <span class="highlight">go.mod</span>, <span class="highlight">Cargo.toml</span>, <span class="highlight">composer.json</span>, <span class="highlight">pom.xml</span>
    &darr;
<span class="highlight">extract-project-conventions.sh</span> detects per-language:
  framework, ORM, auth library, API patterns, naming conventions
    &darr;
Results feed into <span class="highlight">Code Auditor</span> and <span class="highlight">CLAUDE.md generation</span></div>

      <h3>Where language support applies</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>/onboard</code></strong> &mdash; Code Auditor detects language, conventions, frameworks, and test gaps for all 7 languages</li>
        <li><strong><code>/audit</code></strong> &mdash; Security Auditor runs language-specific dependency audit tools and adapts OWASP scanning patterns</li>
        <li><strong>PostToolUse formatting</strong> &mdash; <code>format-code.sh</code> auto-formats with the correct tool: Prettier (JS/TS), Black (Python), rustfmt (Rust), gofmt (Go)</li>
        <li><strong><code>/new-project</code></strong> &mdash; Stack Scout research adapts to the detected or chosen language ecosystem</li>
      </ul>

      <Callout type="info" title="Non-Node.js confidence levels">
        Node.js/TypeScript projects receive &ldquo;high&rdquo; confidence from the Code Auditor due to deeper ecosystem integration. Other languages receive &ldquo;medium&rdquo; confidence &mdash; the core workflow (Tier 1 foundation, Tier 2 cycle, Vibe Score, hooks) works identically, but framework-specific patterns may need manual refinement in CLAUDE.md.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Parallel Backlog Execution ── -->
    <section id="parallel-backlog">
      <h3>Parallel Backlog Execution</h3>
      <p>
        <code>/run-backlog --parallel</code> enables wave-based parallel processing of independent features.
        The system analyzes feature dependencies via <code>analyze-feature-dependencies.sh</code> and groups
        features into waves where no feature depends on another within the same wave.
      </p>
      <div class="agent-table-wrap">
        <table class="agent-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Sequential (default)</th><th>Parallel (<code>--parallel</code>)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Execution</td><td>One feature at a time</td><td>Up to N features per wave (default: 3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>Priority order</td><td>Wave-based: independent features grouped together</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Quality gate</td><td>Per feature</td><td>Per wave (after merge)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost guardrails</td><td>Per feature</td><td>Multiplied by concurrent agents per wave</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Merge strategy</td><td>Sequential on main</td><td>Sequential merge between waves</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h4 class="agent-table-heading">How it works under the hood</h4>
      <p>
        Parallel features are <strong>not</strong> separate Claude Code sessions or terminal tabs. Each feature
        runs as a concurrent <strong>Agent tool call</strong> within the same parent orchestration session.
        Every Agent call gets its own fresh 200k context window and isolated git worktree &mdash; so
        features cannot interfere with each other&apos;s files or accumulate stale context.
      </p>
      <p>
        The parent session stays lightweight: it manages wave ordering, monitors Agent completion,
        runs the quality gate after each wave merges, and advances to the next wave. The
        <code>analyze-feature-dependencies.sh</code> script performs a topological sort on the
        <code>depends_on</code> fields to compute which features can safely run in the same wave.
      </p>

      <Callout type="info" title="When to use parallel mode">
        Use <code>--parallel</code> when your backlog has 3+ features with few dependencies between them.
        For backlogs with chain dependencies (A&rarr;B&rarr;C), parallel mode adds no benefit and falls back to sequential.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Agent Overlay System ── -->
    <section id="agent-overlays">
      <h3>Agent Overlay System</h3>
      <p>
        Customize any agent&apos;s behavior per-project by creating override files in
        <code>.vibecrew/agent-overrides/&lt;agent&gt;.md</code>. Override sections with matching
        <code>## Heading</code> names replace the base section; new headings are appended. YAML
        frontmatter (model, tools, isolation) is always preserved from the base agent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Overrides survive plugin updates because they live in your project, not the plugin.
        See the <a href="personalization.html#agent-overlays">Personalization page</a> for details.
      </p>
    </section>

